  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 247 AM MST Fri Nov 13 2026/ \n\nPatchy fog that developed overnight along the Milk River Valley in \nHill and Blaine counties will persist through this morning before\ndissipating. Visibility will be locally reduced to less than 1/2 \nmile and a Dense Fog Advisory is in effect through 11 AM. \n\nAn amplified upper level pattern remains in place with ridging \ncentered just east of the region today, gradually shifting further \neast across the north-central US this weekend as Pacific troughing \nmoves onshore and initially undercuts the ridge. The bulk of the \nPacific energy moving onshore this weekend stays south of the \narea, but a weakening frontal boundary and and dissipating plume \nof moisture shift across the area Saturday with some light \nprecipitation possible, primarily across southwest MT. While \ntemperatures remain well above seasonal norms through Monday, \nthanks to continued mild flow aloft from the south to southwest, \ntoday will likely be the warmest with increasing cloud-cover and \nslight cooling aloft as the ridge shifts further east. \n\nModel guidance continues to point toward a more active period of \nweather beginning Tuesday as larger scale Pacific troughing shifts \ninland. More widespread precipitation chances and a turn toward cooler \n(seasonable) temperatures develops with the initial wave lifting \nout of the larger scale trough and across the area late Tuesday. \nIn addition to the increased precipitation chances, enough cooling\nlooks to occur with lowering snow levels to bring some risk for \nsnow accumulation at least to some mountain passes and above with\na smaller risk for lower elevation snow by early Wednesday. There\nis still considerable spread among model ensembles with the \nevolution and track of the energy and moisture next week with the \nmain message at this point being a likely turn to more seasonable \ntemperatures and increased chance for precipitation after an \nextended dry/mild period. Hoenisch&&",
